= Working in This Industry After Graduation

The honest answer to the guideline's final question — would I consider
working in this industry after graduation? — is that I have already
signed the contract to go back.

I want to be precise about what that decision is and is not. The
internship ended in late August 2025; the formal return offer and
the signed contract followed some months later. As of writing I do
*not* yet know which team I will join, what my day-to-day
responsibilities will look like, or which technical field I will end
up working in. The decision was therefore made about Google itself,
not about any particular project I have lined up.

The things that drew me back, in roughly the order they mattered:

- *The style of work and the rigour behind it.* Rigorous code review,
  written design culture, and the expectation that the underlying
  structure of a problem is allowed to drive the engineering. This
  is the style I want to spend the early part of my career inside.

- *The opportunities to learn.* Three months in I had had to engage
  with ARM architecture, an open-source C++ codebase tuned for very
  tall relations, the LLVM symbolizer, a clock-domain alignment
  problem, Perfetto SQL, and the design-document discipline that
  ties all of that together. I expect the rate of learning to stay
  this high, and that is on its own enough of a reason to go back.

- *The environment and the culture.* The people I worked with set
  a standard of patience, generosity and seriousness that I want to
  keep being measured against, and the day-to-day environment was
  consistently positive in a way that I had not taken for granted
  going in.

- *London and the offer itself.* The city and the compensation are
  both real parts of the decision. It would be coy to leave them
  out.

A separate question — and one that genuinely is not settled — is
whether further studies will sit on top of this. Google sponsors
graduate study for some employees, and that is an option I am
holding open. Whether I pursue it depends on conversations I still
need to have with people who have walked that path before me, and
I want to make that decision from inside the full-time role rather
than from outside it.

So: for now, yes. For the medium term, I will know more once a
team is assigned and the day-to-day shape of the work is in view.
